// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: ats-v, arch_module, attributes
//
// Pin (T0755 sweep) — the ATS-V attribute parser reads `at_tier` in the
// form the standard library actually writes.
//
//     at_tier: Tier.MultiTier([Tier.Aot, Tier.Interp])
//
//     error: [ATS-V] @arch_module parse error in `…`:
//       InvalidValue { field: "at_tier",
//                      expected: "identifier path or string literal" }
//
// `parse_tier` DID handle `MultiTier` — through the `Call` expression kind. But
// `X.Y(args)` parses as a MethodCall, not a Call, so the arm never fired
// and the value fell through to the identifier branch, which refuses a
// call outright. Four files under `core/security/zk/**` write this form;
// all four failed `verum check` while the bake stayed green.
//
// The MethodCall shape was no discovery: `parse_capability`, in the same
// file, handles it and its comment calls it "the surface used by
// `@arch_module(...)` declarations". Nine sites in that file know about
// MethodCall. One rule, several implementations, one missing the arm.
//
// HOW IT HID. The diagnostic carries NO error code. A sweep filtering on
// `error<` counted these files as clean; `check_core_compiles.sh`, which
// reads the process exit status, called them broken. Two instruments,
// two answers, and the coded-error filter was the one that lied.

@arch_module(
    foundation: Foundation.ZfcTwoInacc,
    stratum: MsfsStratum.LCls,
    lifecycle: Lifecycle.Definition,
    at_tier: Tier.MultiTier([Tier.Aot, Tier.Interp])
)
module spec.architecture.multi_tier;

public fn answer() -> Int {
    42
}

fn main() {
    let _ = answer();
    print("ok");
}
